{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-universal-braid-branching","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Proposition","doco:Section"],"label":"prop:universal-braid-branching","kind":"proposition","title":"Universal braid character formula via Young's lattice branching","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-figure-eight-branching-rule","papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/rem-body-asymptotics-catalog"],"cites":["references/hoefsmit1974","references/wenzl1988"],"tags":["branching-rule","youngs-lattice","universal","borromean","figure-eight","chebyshev","convergence"],"leanRef":"qou:QOU.BraidKnot.universal_braid_branching","text":"universal-braid-branching.md","leanSource":"universal-braid-branching.lean","provenance":"authored","html":"<p><a id=\"prop:universal-braid-branching\"></a>\n<strong>Proposition.</strong> <em>Universal braid character formula via Young's lattice branching</em></p>\n<p><em>For any braid <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">K</code> on <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">3</code> strands and any partition <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\lambda \\vdash k</code> with <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">k \\geq 3</code>, the Frobenius character of <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">K</code> in the irreducible representation <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\rho_\\lambda</code> of <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">H_k(R, q)</code> is:</em></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-math math-display\">  \\chi(K, \\lambda)\n  = f^{\\lambda/[3]} \\cdot \\chi_K^{\\mathrm{sym}}\n  + f^{\\lambda/[2{,}1]} \\cdot \\chi_K^{\\mathrm{std}}\n  + f^{\\lambda/[1^3]} \\cdot \\chi_K^{\\mathrm{alt}}</code></pre>\n<p><em>where <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">f^{\\lambda/\\mu} = |\\mathrm{SYT}(\\lambda/\\mu)|</code> counts saturated chains from <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mu</code> to <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\lambda</code> in Young's lattice. The three base characters compute once from the Hoefsmit seminormal form:</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\"><em>Knot <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">K</code></em></th>\n<th align=\"center\"><em><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\chi_K^{\\mathrm{sym}}</code></em></th>\n<th align=\"center\"><em><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\chi_K^{\\mathrm{std}}</code></em></th>\n<th align=\"center\"><em><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\chi_K^{\\mathrm{alt}}</code></em></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"><em><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">4_1</code> (figure-eight)</em></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><em><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1</code></em></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><em><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2 - [3]_q</code></em></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><em><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1</code></em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"><em><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">B_3</code> (Borromean)</em></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><em><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1</code></em></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><em><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(2 - [3])([3]^2 - 4[3] + 1)</code></em></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><em><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1</code></em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"><em>unknot (identity)</em></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><em><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1</code></em></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><em><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2</code></em></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><em><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1</code></em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Archimedean specialisation.</strong> The character values <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\chi^{\\mathrm{sym}}, \\chi^{\\mathrm{alt}}</code> and skew-SYT counts are stated as real numbers, forming the archimedean evaluations of the generic <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{Chebyshev}/\\mathfrak{sl}_2</code> branching over <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">[\\mathrm{CommRing}\\ R]</code>.</p>\n"}
